llama.cpp (as fetched by Ollama v0.35.1, llama.cpp b11232, applied after
137): flash_attn_combine_results writes 0 for a query row whose keys are
all masked, as the CPU backend does, instead of 0/0.

WHY
---
When a flash-attention kernel splits a head's keys over several blocks,
each block writes a partial result with its running maximum and sum, and
flash_attn_combine_results divides the weighted partial results by the
weighted sums. A row whose keys are all masked has a sum of 0 in every part,
so the combine computes 0/0 and writes NaN. The CPU backend writes 0 for
such a row (S_inv = S == 0 ? 0 : 1/S), and so do the kernels when they do
not split. test-backend-ops caught it on gfx803 with patch 130's prompt
kernel: head size 128, 113 keys, a 512-query batch over two sequences,
split because so few blocks fill the GPU, failed with NaN against the CPU's
0 when it ran inside the full suite (alone it passed). In a model's
prompt only padding rows are masked completely, and their output is not
used, so no model run is known to be affected.

The combine is generic code; the same 0/0 is reached on any GPU whose
kernel splits a fully masked row.

WHAT
----
The combine writes 0 when the weighted sum of a row is 0.

Verified on an RX 590: test-backend-ops test -b ROCm0 -o FLASH_ATTN_EXT,
with the head-96/128/256 prompt cases added, passes 6,555 of 6,555 (6,554
without the patch: the case above). With the K/V split forced off for every
case, the same suite passed without the patch, which places the NaN in the
split path.

diff --git a/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/fattn-common.cuh b/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/fattn-common.cuh
--- a/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/fattn-common.cuh
+++ b/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/fattn-common.cuh
@@ -969,7 +969,9 @@
         VKQ_denominator += KQ_max_scale * meta[l].y;
     }
 
-    dst[tid] = VKQ_numerator / VKQ_denominator;
+    // A row whose keys are all masked has l == 0 in every part; the CPU backend writes 0 for it, and 0/0 here
+    // would write NaN.
+    dst[tid] = VKQ_denominator > 0.0f ? VKQ_numerator / VKQ_denominator : 0.0f;
 }
 
 template <int DV, int ncols1, int ncols2>
